00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.150
So if you read the statement
about Cloud Spanner,

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.250
we are a relational
database server

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.650
that provides strongly
consistent actions,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.730
and that scales horizontally.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.790
Now, that sounds
pretty awesome, right?

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.120
So if you look at
these things separate,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.550
you have that around today
in many, many phases.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.240
If you look at strongly
consistent databases,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.190
there are relational
databases out there

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.840
for 40 years-plus, very
hardened and everything.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.940
But they don't have
the scalability aspect.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.620
Now, if you take
the transactional,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.920
the consistency away,
you have for 10-plus--

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.220
15-plus years, the so-called
[INAUDIBLE] scale databases

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.530
around that give you the
horizontal scalability,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.519
but you do have to sacrifice
on the consistency.

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:13.560
Now, Cloud Spanner tries
to combine these things,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.070
and I'm going to talk about
how we are doing this.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.670
So let's start with
an architecture recap.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.566
First of all, about
the terminology--

00:03:22.566 --> 00:03:23.940
there are a couple
of things that

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.960
are very familiar to people that
work with relational databases.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.630
So we have an instance, which
basically gives your resource

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.690
pool, your nodes that you
have and how much data

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.550
you can store in an instance.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.510
Then you can store, I
think, up to 100 databases

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.890
in an instance.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:45.420
And in each database, you can
store up to 2,048, I believe,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.200
tables.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.120
Now, these are the
constructs that

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.917
are visible to everybody
outside, if you use Spanner.

00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:52.500
And those are the
concepts that you're

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.390
used to if you work with
relational database.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.270
Now, to provide the
scalability in Cloud Spanner,

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:02.320
we have another construct
which is hidden from you.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.760
And that is called a split.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.640
So basically, what
we are doing--

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.000
all the tables are
ordered lexicographically

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.810
by the primary key.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:14.820
And then, we divide them into
chunks that we call split.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.420
And that allows us to scale.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.529
We can basically hand these
chunks between the resource

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:21.570
nodes that we have
available, and make

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.840
them responsible for
different splits.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.340
And by that, we get the
scalability that we see.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.110
So if you look at an
architectural view

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.770
of a regional instance,
you have three zones

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.575
for a regional instance, and
you have your compute resources

00:04:40.575 --> 00:04:41.700
and your storage resources.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.580
Now, they are
independent scalable.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.960
We have some
restrictions, how many

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.050
nodes you have to
have, at least,

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.430
for a certain amount of data.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:55.510
So per two terabyte, you
need at least one node.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:59.250
So you cannot scale further
down than this restriction.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.770
But other than that, you can
scale to thousands of nodes,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.770
even if you have only a
couple of terabytes of data.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.580
Now, in your zones, you
have basically the compute.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.660
For each node that you buy
from us in the Cloud console,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:18.550
you get this node three times
replicated, once in each zone.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.760
And then you have the
storage which, again, is also

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.400
replicated, which is
not illustrated here

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.570
in this graphic.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:29.580
But storage is also replicated
to I don't know how many

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.970
nines of durability.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:37.110
Now, one thing that we need
to basically organize and have

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.450
this strong,
consistent system is

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.510
something to
basically synchronize

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:46.080
who is responsible for a
certain part of your data.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.240
And we never, never,
never want to have

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.310
two compute resources be
responsible for the same data.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.650
Because that is where
inconsistencies come in.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.420
So we needed some
kind of mechanism

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.800
to make sure that we never have
two owners of a data piece.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.580
Now, one way to do this
is with communication.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.560
But communication, especially
if you go further away from each

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.950
other-- so if these zones are
a couple of milliseconds apart

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.150
from each other--

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.200
you basically incur round trips.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.160
So if you look at-- for
additional databases,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.670
they ensure consistency with
the two-phase permit protocol.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.290
And for the two-phase
commit protocol,

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.720
you need multiple
round trips basically

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.980
to ensure this consistency.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.840
Now, that is very expensive and
it wouldn't scale, obviously.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.800
So we needed another way
to ensure the consistency

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.850
while being able to remove
this additional communication.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.540
And the way that we did this
is that we invented TrueTime

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.030
with the Spanner system.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.970
So what is TrueTime here?

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.920
So now, if you look
at TrueTime, TrueTime

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.550
is basically a system
that quantifies

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.500
the worst possible error,
or drift, between clocks

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.740
all over the world.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:00.520
So in all our data centers, we
have so-called GPS [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.050
and time [INAUDIBLE],,
atomic clocks, which

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.870
are synchronized through GPS.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.370
And then, we can quantify
how much they drift, at most.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:16.720
And by this, we are able to
basically choose this timestamp

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.090
interval, where we can say that
the lower end of this interval

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:25.020
has passed everywhere, and
the upper end of this interval

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.440
has passed nowhere.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.660
So you see there, if I
read the TrueTime now

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.660
returns in interval
T1 and T2, where

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:38.030
T2 is T1, so the lower
bound plus 2 epsilon, which

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:39.890
is basically our
security, in terms

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.480
of the drift that we expect.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.420
And then, T1 is
guaranteed to be lower

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.790
than the value of the
global clock at the instance

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.440
when now finishes executing.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.280
So when you get this interval,
this has definitely passed.

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.740
And T2 is guaranteed
to be higher

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.590
than when this [INAUDIBLE]
now starts executing.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.810
So we use this
interval-- this TrueTime

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.900
interval-- to ensure
strong consistency

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.100
across the database.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.800
And not only within one region.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.250
We can do that across
the regions, even.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.340
So let's look at a little
bit about how does it work.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.480
Let's start with a
simple life of a query.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.820
So here, in this case, I
want to do a consistent read,

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.880
so I basically want to get the
latest state of my database.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.185
And we choose here
a select statement,

00:08:28.185 --> 00:08:31.990
so I select from Event,
where the name is Next 2018.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.390
Now, the request comes
in from your client.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.340
It goes through a
follower because that's

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.437
the one that is closest--

00:08:39.437 --> 00:08:40.520
the follower of the split.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.539
So you basically
have the Paxos group,

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.920
where you choose a leader, and
then you have two followers.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.480
And then the leader
is basically who

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.830
holds the hold-throughs of
your data for that split.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.920
Now, the request comes
into your follower,

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:59.420
and the follower sends a request
to the leader of this Paxos

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.120
group and says, hey, do
I have the latest data?

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.560
And if the leader
sends back and says,

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.830
yes, this timestamp
is what I have.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:09.920
If you have this timestamp,
you have the latest data,

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.700
the follower serves
the data back.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.870
So there's no data going between
the leader and follower here.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.110
It's really just to check,
do I have the latest data?

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:22.970
Now, in the case that the data
is not there yet, basically

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:27.082
the leader responds saying, hey,
you don't have the latest data.

00:09:27.082 --> 00:09:28.790
This is the timestamp
of the latest data.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:31.919
Please wait that out till
you see the timestamp

00:09:31.919 --> 00:09:33.710
And once you see it,
you can serve the data

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:37.440
because then you know that
you have the latest data.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:37.940
Right.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.850
We have, also, another
construct in Spanner,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.510
which we use for optimization,
especially if you

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.410
use a global
instance and you want

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.390
to have fast response times.

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:50.660
You can do something which
is called time-bounded reads.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.850
And time-bounded reads
are not consistent--

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:53.960
not strong consistent.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.070
They are consistent.

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.900
They are consistent
time-bounded reads,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.890
but they are not the
latest ones, basically.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:03.620
Now, for a time-bounded
read, so-called stale read,

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.150
you can define the boundary.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.940
You can say, OK, I accept
data, which is up to,

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.770
let's say, 15 seconds old.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:13.145
Or you can actually
define an instance of time

00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:14.360
within one hour.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.340
So we allow up to
one hour that you

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.310
can go back in time with
a time-bounded read.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:22.770
And in the time-bounded read,
in many, many, many, many, many,

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.380
many, many cases--

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.990
especially if you use a
15-second time bound--

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:31.130
the data is already
there for your follower.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.940
Because if we have
a transaction, not

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.000
directory as part of the
transaction, but immediately,

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.600
when this transaction
is happening,

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.590
we are sending out that
data to all the followers.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.940
So they have that
actually pretty quickly.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.690
So here, in this case, if we do
a request and we say we allow

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:50.810
15-seconds-old data, the
follower basically checks,

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:51.940
do I have--

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.690
if the data hasn't
changed for a while,

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.690
we basically send a
heartbeat to update,

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.420
this is the latest data.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.075
Then we can say,
OK, I have the data.

00:11:01.075 --> 00:11:02.450
I don't need to
go to the leader.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:06.020
I can directly serve that data,
and I leave the leader alone,

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.040
which is pretty amazing.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.750
Now, I want to look into writes.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:15.530
So I mentioned one thing
is two-phase commit

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.400
is something that is expensive.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:21.050
Another thing which
is expensive is locks.

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:26.320
So Spanner was designed in a
way you only use what you need.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.800
So if you don't
need certain things,

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.780
you don't pay the
penalty of, for instance,

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:33.050
a two-phase commit.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.800
So as an example, if you go
into a traditional database

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.870
and you want to have--

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:40.977
you do a transaction, but
you only read something

00:11:40.977 --> 00:11:41.810
in this transaction.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:43.599
You don't actually
write something.

00:11:43.599 --> 00:11:45.140
You still acquire
locks and you still

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:49.490
have certain two-phase commits
in some of the systems.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.320
And you basically pay
the penalty for that,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:55.070
even though you have maybe
not even changed any data.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:58.970
Now, in Spanner, we allow for
certain kinds of transactions

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:01.684
to actually be
lockless, and we allow

00:12:01.684 --> 00:12:03.100
for certain kinds
of transactions,

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:06.200
actually, for
locks to be shared.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:09.380
So here's an example of
a single split write.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:10.670
In a single-split write--

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:13.610
let's say it's a point
update, and let's also

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.230
say it's a blind update.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:16.580
So I'm not reading anything.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:19.460
I just say, this
value, with key 100,

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.880
I want to write A. As you
see, on the left side,

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.620
I want to write, for
key 100, the value A.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.000
So what's happening,
basically, this comes in,

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.910
let's say, through
the API front end.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.640
And we do a split
and leader lookup.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:36.830
Now, this is very
heavily cached.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.530
I left it in here in the graph.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:42.050
But basically, the round-trip
that you see to the zone

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:45.740
master, is rarely, rarely
happening because we are

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.554
caching very heavily
in the front-end,

00:12:47.554 --> 00:12:50.220
where the splits are living, and
where the leaders are for these

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:51.350
splits.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:56.330
So we basically get back
who the split leader is.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:13:01.130
So we're sending, to the split
leader, the commit request.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.707
And I would actually
change the slide right now,

00:13:03.707 --> 00:13:04.790
because there is an error.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.280
This send commit request
goes to the split leader,

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:08.420
not to the other replicas.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:10.490
I apologize for that mistake.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.410
Now, what the split
leader does, it

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:15.650
basically acquires a
write lock, and then

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:18.590
assigns a TrueTime timestamp
waits out that time.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.830
While waiting out this time,
we send a persist request

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.230
to the other replicas.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:27.540
As soon as we get the
majority of acknowledge

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.290
that this change was
persisted, we basically say,

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.665
OK, quorum is reached.

00:13:32.665 --> 00:13:36.140
We just wait out the
through time upper bound.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.330
We know, at that
time, it's persistent,

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:41.450
and then we send back,
to the front-end,

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:45.000
or to the client, that the
transaction was successful.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.400
Now, I mentioned this
is a blind write.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:52.130
And in a blind write, we can
actually share the write locks.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.220
So if you have multiple
blind write transactions,

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.630
they can share the write
lock, which is amazing.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:01.340
So you can actually just use
TrueTime for the ordering,

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.170
and they are not
conflicting, and we

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:04.700
can share the write locks.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:05.390
All right.

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:07.950
So let's make that a little
bit more complicated.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.150
Now, in this case, we
have a multi-split write.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:15.170
So here, what I'm doing
is I'm reading the value

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:16.820
from the key 100.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.580
I'm writing a value to
key 100, the value A.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:24.760
I'm writing, to the key 300,
the value B. And to the key 400,

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.720
the value C.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:32.600
Now again, we do the split
leader lookup for our read

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.120
because that's the first
thing that we send.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:37.340
Now, we send the read
request to the split leader

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:39.740
of the zone A, split zero.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:44.240
That is where my key value
100 lives, and the leader is.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:46.800
And the leader
acquires a read lock--

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:49.420
read locks, again,
can be shared--

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.650
and sends the result back.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:53.960
Now, on my client,
I do something,

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:55.460
and then I'm sending the writes.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:00.080
I'm sending the write for
100, for 300, and 400.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:01.732
So before I send
that, of course,

00:15:01.732 --> 00:15:04.190
I have to do the lookup, where
the leaders for these values

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:05.210
live.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.420
Then I send the commit
requests to my split leader

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:15.010
of the zone A split zero, which
is my key 100 split leader.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:20.260
Then I send it to zone C, split
two, which is my key 300 zone

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:21.350
and split leader.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:24.320
And then to zone
B and split three,

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:29.190
which is my zone
leader for the key 400.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.700
Now, what the front-end
is doing, as well,

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:34.500
is when we do this
writing section,

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:39.980
it is choosing a leader of
this multi-split transaction.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.690
We need, basically,
a leader who is

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:43.860
in control of this transaction.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.460
Now, since it is
multi-split, we actually

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.190
have to do two-phase commit.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.950
In the single write, we didn't
have to do two-phase commit.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:54.260
But since this is
multi-split, we actually

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:57.050
have to coordinate between
the different Paxos groups,

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.580
so we have to do have
a two-phase commit.

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:02.150
So when we attempt
the commit request,

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:06.470
each of the split leaders
is requesting their locks.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:11.210
So I basically do the write lock
for the zone B, a write lock

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:14.870
for the key 300, and I do
also verify the read lock

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.880
for the first one that I did.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:20.120
I am also writing this key,
so I am also requesting

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.440
a write lock for that.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.140
And then, once all these
locks are required,

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.700
I send, basically, that
back to the chosen leader

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:30.570
or coordinator of
this transaction.

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:34.640
So now, what is happening, once
I have all these confirmations

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.920
that my locks are acquired,
and they are still valid,

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.740
the leader basically
assigns a TrueTime

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:43.060
timestamp to that transaction,
records the commit

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:45.440
to some replicas.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.270
And once it gets a majority
of this in their Paxos group,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:53.690
it sends it out to the
other split leaders.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.190
And then the other
split leaders is

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:59.330
recording that commit to the
replicas in their Paxos group.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:02.060
And as soon as they have
the majority, they then--

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:05.720
they basically are
prepared for that commit.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:10.099
And then, once this
is done, basically, we

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:12.410
know that the commit is done.

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:15.317
The first phase was
basically verifying

00:17:15.317 --> 00:17:17.900
that we have all the locks, and
that we can get all the locks.

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:20.069
The second phase here,
you see, is basically,

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:22.230
yes, please commit
that transaction.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:24.910
We have all the locks, so we
can commit this transaction.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:28.700
We again made out the
TrueTime uncertainty,

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.490
and report back
the commit success

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:34.730
to all our other participants
of that transaction.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:39.910
And then, in parallel pretty
much, the mutation is applied.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:44.070
Now, if there's any
point in time where

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:47.660
we have a lock conflict-- for
instance, I acquire read lock,

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:50.070
and another transaction
has a read lock, as well.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:53.271
And then I want to upgrade
that read lock to a write lock.

00:17:53.271 --> 00:17:55.270
But since the other
transaction has a read lock,

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.520
I cannot upgrade it
to the write lock.

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:00.170
What is happening in Spanner
is, basically, we do a wait

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.160
and wound.

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:02.960
So basically, what
it is doing, it

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:07.310
is going to cancel-- abort,
basically-- the transaction

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:08.390
that is younger.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:12.540
So the front-end knows the
ordering of the transaction.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.080
And if they see there's
a looming deadlock,

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:19.350
we are basically aborting
the younger transaction,

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.310
let proceed the
older transaction.

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.920
And then we just retry
the younger transaction

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:26.820
that we aborted.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:27.320
All right.

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:31.370
So with that knowledge,
what are the things

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:35.600
to look out for when
working with Cloud Spanner?

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:39.560
So first and foremost-- and
one of the most straightforward

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:42.320
ones sometimes still missed--

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:45.900
is the choosing of the
right primary keys.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.840
So primary keys, I
mentioned in the beginning,

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.310
the tables are ordered
lexicographically.

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:54.770
Or for the mere mortals,
alphanumerically.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:57.590
So if you have something
which is monotonic

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:00.220
increasing or
decreasing, you basically

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.770
add to the last split,
or to the first split.

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:06.710
And as mentioned,
one split is always

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:09.570
owned by one computer source.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:11.600
So if you always
add to one split,

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:15.680
you basically exhaust the
computer sources on that split.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:19.130
So the recommendation, use
well-distributed primary keys,

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:22.970
or composite primary
keys if you can.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:26.390
Also, look at
reordering your table

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:30.290
if you see that that can
help in getting your key more

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:34.180
distributed-- your component
primary key more distributed.

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:36.740
Another thing is be
careful with interleaving.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.020
So interleaving can be powerful.

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:41.390
Interleaving is a technique
where you basically

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:44.800
make sure that the data lives
on the same split as the parent

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:45.300
table.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.008
So you interleave it
in the parent table.

00:19:47.008 --> 00:19:50.260
They have to share the same
primary key in the beginning,

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:53.960
the same primary key prefix.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:56.480
And then it is
interleaved and stored

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:00.170
on the same split
as your parent row.

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:02.150
Now, the danger is,
if you have one parent

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.270
row and a lot of data which
relates to that parent row,

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:08.360
you basically, again, end
up with all the traffic

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.690
to that data to the child
table in one split, which

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.170
also leads to hotspotting.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:15.170
So be careful with that.

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:18.830
It is a technique to speed up
certain queries, especially

00:20:18.830 --> 00:20:23.420
if you need to do some kinds of
fast lookups for certain data,

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.180
where you want to benefit
from data locality.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.330
You can use interleaving,
but be careful of it.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.340
Same applies to indexes.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:35.030
So indexes are just
tables in Spanner.

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:38.330
So if you index a
monotonic field,

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:39.650
you get in the same problem.

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:41.240
You get also into hotspotting.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:42.590
So be careful there.

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:45.710
Use application-level
sharding key prefix,

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:48.080
or something that is
natural in your table

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:51.420
that you can use to
prefix your indexes.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:54.650
Now, for complex transactions,
one of the things

00:20:54.650 --> 00:21:00.150
is Spanner basically
locks on cell level.

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:05.045
So if you have columns and
rows, the lock level for Spanner

00:21:05.045 --> 00:21:06.990
is on the cell level.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.040
But if you do, for instance,
a read/write transaction,

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:14.030
where you select on
a non-key column,

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:18.290
then your entire table
for that column is locked.

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:20.780
Because we don't know
what is the range,

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:22.160
how many actually it could be.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.160
We also lock for
new rows inserted,

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.560
or for any rows that
are updated, which

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.295
could be qualifying
for that filter

00:21:29.295 --> 00:21:32.210
that you set on
a non-key column.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:34.890
So we're basically
locking the entire column.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.150
And with this, you could
run into, basically,

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:40.190
contention for your writes.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:42.020
So be careful with this.

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.930
A good strategy to do
this is, if you can,

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:46.820
use a primary key
in your filter,

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.250
so that you basically
can filter down

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:51.980
on the primary key, which
allows that we don't

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:54.320
do a full-column table lock.

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:56.630
And the other thing
is, if you can,

00:21:56.630 --> 00:22:00.086
do lookups or filtering
before you do actually

00:22:00.086 --> 00:22:01.210
the read/write transaction.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:04.760
So a good technique is doing
a read-only transaction

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:06.780
before your read/write
transaction,

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:09.590
get the data that you
really want to modify.

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:12.290
Get the primary keys of
that, and then use that,

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:14.930
and verify that the
data hasn't changed

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:17.120
in your read/write transaction.

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:20.330
And then you have a much smaller
scope of your transaction,

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:23.271
and by that, you avoid a
lot of the write contention.

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:31.750
So to that point, use read/write
transactions only if needed.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.200
So read-only transactions
are actually lockless.

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:38.940
So read-only transactions just
use the TrueTime timestamp.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:40.590
And we don't do any locks.

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:42.480
We don't do a read
lock or anything.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:45.840
The only time you actually
acquire a read lock

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:48.990
is inside a read/write
transaction, if you do a read.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:53.700
Otherwise, you don't acquire
any locks in your queries,

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:55.410
or transactions.

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:58.960
Now, another thing that we see,
in terms of read contention,

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.400
where you have a lot of data--

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:05.010
hotspotting during
reads-- one of the things

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.780
is if you have popular items.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.860
It could be, for
instance, a counter.

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:13.680
For a counter, we recommend
to shard the counter

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:15.210
if you need one.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.540
It could be a popular item.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:19.920
Let's say you have a social
graph in your Spanner database,

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:23.490
and you have that star that
everybody looks up and wants

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.800
to get the latest news.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:29.460
So to avoid these kinds
of hot data points,

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:34.410
one technique that you can do
is replicating during writes.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:38.520
So basically, when you
write, you do replicas,

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:42.510
and then you enable the
read from these replicas.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:45.120
These writes are
transactional, so you don't

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:48.360
get the inconsistency in there.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:50.430
Or if you can look
at your schema,

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:53.580
if you can do things
like reordering columns

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.670
to avoid these hot data spots.

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:59.400
Another thing, indexes.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.950
Consider how you
define your indexes.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:04.970
Also, the same is
for primary keys.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:08.840
Spanner always scans from
the top of the table, OK?

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:12.030
So if you have your
index order ascending,

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:14.940
and you ask for that
index for-- let's say,

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:22.200
you have an index on updated
and some app level sharding

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:23.730
to avoid hotspotting.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:26.010
And you want to have
the latest updates

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:27.300
on certain kinds of data.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.380
And you have your index
defined descending.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.280
The index doesn't help you.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:36.120
Define your index descending
so that the newest values are

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:39.510
at the top, and you don't
need to scan the entire index.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:43.620
Another important thing--
since Cloud Spanner

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:46.860
is a distributed database,
we don't have optimizations

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.340
that you find in single
machine databases.

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.250
So if you look at indexes in
a single machine relational

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:56.310
database, the way that
indexes are implemented--

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:59.220
if they are implemented
performantly--

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:01.650
is that they are actually
pointing directly

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.770
to the data point on
your disk, or in memory,

00:25:04.770 --> 00:25:06.739
where that data lives.

00:25:06.739 --> 00:25:08.280
Now, in Spanner,
that is not possible

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:11.220
because that data could
live on a different node.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:14.160
The index that you have
might be in a different split

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:15.660
than the data,
actually, that you're

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:18.370
accessing with your query.

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:20.430
So the only thing
that we can do here

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:25.710
is we do actually have to do a
back join to your parent table.

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:28.830
Now, to avoid this, what you
can do in your index definition

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:31.890
is using something
like STORING clause.

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.350
So the STORING clause are
columns that are not indexed,

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.530
but that you basically attach to
your index that will be carried

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:41.420
around with your index,
and might avoid a back join

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:44.500
into your parent table.

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:47.010
Now, again, the
things that we also

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:48.840
discussed during
the write contention

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.360
apply also to read contention.

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.660
Consider reordering columns
for better distribution.

00:25:54.660 --> 00:26:01.830
Introduce app-level sharding
to basically avoid hotspots.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:03.750
And then, the other
thing is always

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:08.340
to keep in mind
use indexes wisely.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:12.120
So indexes are coming with
an improved read performance,

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:14.862
but at the cost of
reduced write because they

00:26:14.862 --> 00:26:16.070
are spanning multiple splits.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:18.480
So pretty much, if you
have an index defined,

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:20.490
unless it's an
interleaved index,

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:23.910
you always will have a two-phase
commit during your writes

00:26:23.910 --> 00:26:26.280
because you have
other splits involved

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:28.920
that need to be updated, OK?

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.800
So choose them wisely.

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:34.730
Not always the indexes help.

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:35.400
OK?

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:37.490
And they also come at
the storage overhead,

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:40.350
so this is also
something to consider.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.950
Now, if you look at schema
design and life cycles, one

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:45.400
of the things that we see--

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:48.660
especially if you look at
a continuous integration

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.930
and delivery setup--

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:57.320
one thing that is done
quite often, actually--

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:58.560
scarily often--

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:02.340
is to do schema changes
with continuous delivery.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:04.620
And if you deploy
multiple times--

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:10.680
maybe even during
development especially--

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:12.750
if you deploy multiple
times per minute,

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:16.140
for instance, you get all these
schema changes per minute.

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.950
And now what happens
is actually--

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:19.430
Spanner is built in a way--

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:23.746
Cloud Spanner is built in
a way to serve live traffic

00:27:23.746 --> 00:27:25.750
while schema
changes are ongoing.

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:28.960
So our biggest
primary concern is

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:32.250
we want to serve live
traffic while we also

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:34.670
allow online schema changes.

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.990
So we basically take a hit.

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:43.080
We accept the hit on the schema
changes to be propagated.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:46.590
But for that, you get the
live serving of your traffic.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.150
Now, there's also another thing.

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:52.340
The schema changes
are done sequentially.

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:54.340
There are preparation
phases, which

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:58.040
are done in parallel while
your traffic is going on.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:28:01.370
And then, at the point that
we do with the switchover,

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:04.560
we choose a TrueTime timestamp,
they are transactional.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.250
We choose a TrueTime timestamp.

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:08.610
At the point that we
switch over the schema,

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.870
we are aborting all
the transactions

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:13.380
that are in flight at that
point that would conflict,

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:16.520
which basically would span
two versions of the schema.

00:28:16.520 --> 00:28:17.980
Now, another point
to consider is

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:21.640
we are keeping schema changes
in memory for about an hour,

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:22.900
a little longer.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.650
The reason that we are
doing this is we're

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:26.710
allowing time-bounded reads.

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:29.590
And in time-bounded reads, you
can basically go back in time.

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:31.900
And by going back in
time, you could actually

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:33.800
hit an old schema.

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.710
So we still need
to be able to serve

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:39.670
the traffic for that old
schema for time-bounded reads.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:40.760
So keep that in mind.

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:44.860
It is basically incurring
memory pressure on our nodes

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:49.010
because it's taking up space
if you do a schema change.

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:53.290
And so we are basically lowering
the amount of schema changes

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.280
that you can do per time unit.

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:59.290
So really consider
that when you're doing

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:02.740
schema updates, that you
don't do them too often

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:06.060
in a short amount of time.

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:09.240
Also, one thing to
mention is, if you

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:14.310
create indexes, for instance,
in your schema changes,

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:15.360
of course--

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:17.130
as in other databases--

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:21.930
they need to be back-filled,
especially in Spanner

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:23.880
because they are
just normal tables.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:26.554
We need to back-fill
these indexes.

00:29:26.554 --> 00:29:28.470
Depending on the amount
of data that you have,

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:33.930
that can take minutes, hours,
or sometimes even days,

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:37.950
if you have a lot of indexes
that you need to back-fill.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:39.300
All right.

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:41.390
A couple of things on
the application design.

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:45.010
We introduced recently
the commit timestamps.

00:29:45.010 --> 00:29:49.169
So you can now add a
column to your schema

00:29:49.169 --> 00:29:51.210
automatically, where you
can say, in your client,

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.760
please fill that column
with the commit timestamp.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.160
You can use that, for instance,
if you update any data,

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:59.440
like [INAUDIBLE] this
be automatically filled.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:01.500
I put it down in Java and Go.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.280
We have that for many, many
other languages, as well.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:08.820
You see there, you
basically define a constant

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.370
that you provide in
your write transaction,

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:14.170
and it will be automatically
filled on the back-end

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:16.860
with the value of the timestamp.

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:20.430
One important thing is also
to benefit from caching,

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.840
from query caching.

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:26.640
And to avoid, of course,
SQL injection attacks.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:28.580
Please use bounded parameters.

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:30.840
These allow you to
basically-- and allow us

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:32.940
to do more efficient caching.

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:36.630
And by that, basically,
lowering also response times

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:41.000
because we don't need to create
a new query plan every time.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:42.330
All right.

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:48.110
In terms of bulk data imports
and exports, on the one hand,

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:51.300
we have Apache Beam
connector, which

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:55.080
is doing all the hard
work for you to optimize

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.990
the export and import of data.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:02.210
We also have, just recently--
last week, I believe--

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:04.800
announced that we have
import and export built

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:06.300
into Cloud Console.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:08.550
So what you can do now is
you just click a button.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:12.480
You say your Google Cloud
Storage destination, and say,

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:13.500
I want to export there.

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:16.080
And then, you can import
from there, as well.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.460
Very long-awaited feature
for many of our customers.

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:22.200
So we're really happy that
this is now available,

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:25.390
and it will actually
improve over time, as well.

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:29.310
Now, if you are
somebody like me,

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:32.820
who wants to actually understand
and do all the bulk loading,

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:36.190
and importing and
exporting yourself,

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:38.460
there is a trick to get
the maximum throughput

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.980
for your imports and exports,
especially for the imports,

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.310
to avoid the write contention.

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:45.960
I have a video
series around that,

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:48.030
so please feel free
to follow up on that.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:52.230
But basically, what you do is
you load a large amount of rows

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:53.370
into cache.

00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:55.990
You sort these rows
by your primary key.

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:57.260
That can be a composited key.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:58.695
You're basically
sort it by that.

00:31:58.695 --> 00:32:04.800
Then you split them into
chunks, into distinct ranges

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:06.570
of your primary key chunks.

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:09.100
And then you send those
chunks in parallel.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.820
Now, somebody might
say, wait, didn't you

00:32:11.820 --> 00:32:16.420
say not monotonic increasing
and decreasing primary key?

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:18.210
Why are you sorting?

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:21.660
The reason here is, you're
sorting and sending in parallel

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:24.030
all these chunks,
which basically means

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:26.010
they go to different splits.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:28.170
There might be some overlap.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:30.380
But Spanner will recognize
that, during time,

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:33.070
we do load-balanced
optimizations,

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:36.990
load-balanced splitting,
load-balanced handover

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:41.310
of responsibilities for
splits during your traffic,

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:42.750
so we can actually
optimize that.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:47.460
And since you send a bunch, or
many, of these sorted ranges

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:51.160
to Spanner, we can actually
get a really high throughput.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:55.950
So I was able to get
a, I think, 12x speedup

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:59.710
by just using that
technique of importing data.

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:02.430
Another thing which
we recently added

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:07.050
is tracing, which is integrated
directly into Stackdriver.

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:09.880
And I'm going to demonstrate
that in a little bit.

00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:13.110
So basically that allows
you to get some insights

00:33:13.110 --> 00:33:15.480
into where is your query low?

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:19.020
Is it actually Spanner that
is taking a lot of time?

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:22.370
Is it maybe on your client
side, which uses a lot of time,

00:33:22.370 --> 00:33:24.750
and you can actually
optimize on the client side?

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:28.560
So Open Census allows you
to do some tracing there.

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:32.160
And we actually-- if you get
these IDs for your tracers--

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:34.030
they go back into our system.

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:37.110
So we actually can
trace certain queries

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:40.090
that we have found that
are slow on our side.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:44.400
We can actually use the UID, and
trace them back in our system

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:46.830
and see what was going on.

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:48.720
So that is actually
pretty powerful.

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:51.440
I encourage everybody to
check it out and use it,

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:54.390
and I'm going to show
it in a little bit.

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:56.100
All right.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:58.980
Let me actually show
that the first time.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.550
So I'm going to switch
over to my demo laptop.

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:09.340
So basically, how
are you setting up

00:34:09.340 --> 00:34:10.900
tracing in Cloud Spanner?

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:14.469
I hope it's-- is that visible,
or should I make that a little

00:34:14.469 --> 00:34:16.889
bigger?

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:19.199
Let me get that away.

00:34:19.199 --> 00:34:19.699
All right.

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:23.040
So basically, to get
Open Census tracing--

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:24.139
this is in Go.

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:28.870
I apologize for anybody
who's not a Go person.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:31.250
But currently, my
favorite language.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:35.090
So here in Stackdriver, you
basically define a new exporter

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:36.820
with some Stackdriver options.

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:38.830
In this case, the
project ID and some

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.900
attributes that you define.

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:44.739
I basically attached some
labels to my tracers.

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:47.440
I want to have the
demo that is running,

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:50.510
and the region where this
is running in this line.

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:54.540
And then, I set up some views
and register that exporter.

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:57.580
So that basically is
all the setup here.

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:00.610
Last but not least, we apply
a config for this trace

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:03.070
and say, OK, how
many traces do I want

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:06.790
to get from all my requests?

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:11.080
So you see here, I want to have
1% of my requests as traced,

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:14.470
and then these are
sent to Stackdriver.

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:20.320
Now, how does it look
if I do my actual query?

00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:22.720
So in my actual query,
let's say I'm doing

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:25.377
a write for a sequential row.

00:35:25.377 --> 00:35:26.710
So I do a sequential write here.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:32.130
So I define a span by
starting that span.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:34.210
That's just basically
the containment

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:41.170
of my trace for round-trip--
let's say it's that way.

00:35:41.170 --> 00:35:44.740
I'm ending that span when
the function is left.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:46.480
And then I'm using
that context that I'm

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:50.554
using here, and passing that
context down in my library.

00:35:50.554 --> 00:35:51.970
So this is really
important if you

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:54.880
use Go, that you
use the same context

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:58.340
and just wrap the context if
you need some other qualities.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.540
So here, for instance, I'm
doing a context with a timeout,

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:04.540
where I say, if, after five
seconds, it doesn't come back,

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:07.730
please cancel that whole thing.

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:10.690
So I'm wrapping the context
that I'm getting in my function

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:14.740
into this timeout context.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:18.070
And then, at the end,
that's pretty much it.

00:36:18.070 --> 00:36:19.570
I don't have to
do anything else.

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:22.420
All the Open Census,
tracing, is already

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:23.590
supported in the library.

00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:32.620
It's literally these two rows up
here which enable the tracing.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:35.290
Now, how does that look
in my [? Explorer? ?]

00:36:35.290 --> 00:36:38.110
So we can see
here, for instance,

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:40.490
all the traces that
I've collected.

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:43.400
Let's update this and see
if this is still running.

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:45.040
So we see, OK, this
is still running.

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:49.060
It shows you what are the
times here for these traces.

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:51.190
Now you see that
there are pretty much

00:36:51.190 --> 00:36:55.340
three lines in response time.

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:59.350
And the reason for this is,
actually, the most upper line

00:36:59.350 --> 00:37:03.850
is clients running in Asia
against a Cloud Spanner

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:06.730
instance in the US.

00:37:06.730 --> 00:37:08.950
The middle line is
my clients running

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:13.570
in Europe against my Cloud
Spanner instance in the US.

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:19.360
And the lowest one here, which
has very low round-trip times,

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:23.767
is my clients running in the US
in the same region as my Cloud

00:37:23.767 --> 00:37:25.030
Spanner instance.

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:27.160
Now, the great thing
that you can do here

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:31.600
is you can pick one of
those, and show events.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:35.570
And it shows you, OK, I'm
acquiring a read-only session.

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:37.570
I acquired a read-only
session, and then I'm

00:37:37.570 --> 00:37:39.550
going to Spanner
and do the commit.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:42.580
And you see the 200
milliseconds here.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:47.390
Now, let's see if I can
maybe find something else.

00:37:47.390 --> 00:37:49.770
So here you see,
for instance, we

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.050
are iterating-- we're
doing a sequential write.

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:55.150
You see the things
that are going on.

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:57.490
If I'm going a little
bit further down,

00:37:57.490 --> 00:37:59.290
let's see if I can find one.

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:01.240
Yesterday, I found
a really nice one

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:05.720
which showed me
that, for instance,

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:08.020
if your session
pool is too small,

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:10.450
and it needs to
require a session,

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:13.090
then you see it's like, OK,
I'm waiting for a session,

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:14.590
I'm waiting for a session,
I'm waiting for a session.

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:16.090
Oh, now I got a server session.

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:21.580
So the entire query took,
let's say, 500 milliseconds.

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:25.600
But the actual Spanner part
of it was five milliseconds.

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:28.300
The 495 milliseconds
was the client

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:30.190
trying to acquire a session.

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:32.880
And you can fix these
things on your client side.

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:36.160
So you basically get insights
into where is the problem?

00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:38.700
Are my queries slow, or
is actually my client

00:38:38.700 --> 00:38:43.192
not optimized for the things
that I'm doing with it?

00:38:43.192 --> 00:38:44.689
All right.

00:38:44.689 --> 00:38:46.480
So the next thing that
I want to talk about

00:38:46.480 --> 00:38:49.420
is multi-region configurations.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.790
So we have two offers right now.

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:54.640
We have a multi-region
continental,

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:57.010
and we have a
multi-region global.

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:59.560
Both of them give
you a five nines SLA

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:05.130
So the regional instance
gives you a four nine SLA.

00:39:05.130 --> 00:39:08.000
The multi-region gives
you a five nine SLA.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:12.000
Now, what is continental
multi-region instance?

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:15.050
You get a full copy of a
database in two regions.

00:39:15.050 --> 00:39:18.320
You have the Paxos that
I explained earlier

00:39:18.320 --> 00:39:19.930
across three regions.

00:39:19.930 --> 00:39:22.490
I'm going to go a little bit
in more detail in a second.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:26.360
And it is available even
if one of your regions,

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:29.150
or read/write regions,
becomes unavailable.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:32.150
So the same applies for
the multi-region global.

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:34.040
Whereas, for the
multi-region global,

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:38.680
you add two read-only copies
of your full read-only copies

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:41.240
on two more continents.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:45.010
In this case, Europe and Asia.

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.290
Now, how does it look?

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:48.680
For multi-region
continental, you basically

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.580
have a region one,
you have a region two,

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:53.310
and then you have a
so-called witness region.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:55.230
Now, the witness region
has a lock store,

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:57.300
but it doesn't have
a copy of your data.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:40:00.420
And the witness region is
basically somebody who has

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:03.150
voting rights, but
cannot serve data.

00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:05.370
Now, why do we need
this witness region?

00:40:05.370 --> 00:40:07.370
We need it, actually, for
the high availability.

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:11.940
Now, let's assume your region
two becomes unavailable, right?

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:14.460
If you only would have these
two regions in your quorum--

00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:17.220
you cannot really get a quorum.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:19.530
So you could have a split brain.

00:40:19.530 --> 00:40:20.550
You don't want that.

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:22.470
So you need some
kind of third region

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:27.390
to get the majority in your
votings for the Paxos group.

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:30.760
And the witness region
has this function for you.

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:32.880
So now, if region two,
for instance, goes down,

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:35.280
we can still serve
data from region one

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:38.040
because the witness region
helps to have the quorum

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:40.230
for the transactions.

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:43.560
Now, on a global
setup, you basically

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:45.030
have region one read/write.

00:40:45.030 --> 00:40:48.300
You have another
region read/write--

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:49.980
in the US, in this case.

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:53.910
Now, for quorums, again, you
also have a witness region.

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:56.640
Depending how far they
are away from each other,

00:40:56.640 --> 00:41:00.750
you incur their round-trip
times for your transactions,

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:03.780
especially in the
two-phase commit.

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:06.720
And then you have the
read-only regions,

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:10.380
which are on the write
side, in Europe and in Asia.

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:14.710
Now, what do you read-only
regions bring you?

00:41:14.710 --> 00:41:16.800
First, they are
there to serve data.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:18.930
So not always you
have to ship data

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:21.150
from your read/write regions.

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:26.370
Second, if you use stale,
time-bounded consistent reads,

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:29.830
these can be served directly
from your read-only regions

00:41:29.830 --> 00:41:31.540
if we have the
latest data there.

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:35.820
So if you use 10 seconds, you're
pretty much safe to use that.

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:40.080
So in the case of writes
and consistent reads,

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:43.260
you see the blue lines on
the right is a strong read.

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:46.640
Now if we get basically
in from-- a client in Asia

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:52.170
goes into the read-only
copy in Asia and asks,

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:53.740
hey, I want to
have the data, what

00:41:53.740 --> 00:41:58.470
we incur is this round-trip
time to read/write region.

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.660
And ask, hey, do we
have the latest data?

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:04.020
Then we get the response, and
we serve the data from Asia.

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.550
Now, if you have
a write, we don't

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.300
go to the read-only region.

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:10.230
We go directly to the
read/write region,

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:14.160
where the splits live in
the read/write regions.

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:16.860
Now, if a US line comes in,
it's pretty much the same

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:19.050
as you have for regional.

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:20.760
Now, if you have
time-bounded ones,

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:24.390
again, you basically go
to your read-only region.

00:42:24.390 --> 00:42:28.540
If it is within the bounds that
you have that data already,

00:42:28.540 --> 00:42:30.780
you can serve it
without even consulting

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.930
your read/write region.

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:33.450
OK.

00:42:33.450 --> 00:42:35.580
So before I go in
the related session,

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:39.790
I just want to show, my
last couple of minutes,

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:40.790
a couple of things.

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:45.190
So one that I want to do
is I want to start a demo.

00:42:45.190 --> 00:42:46.350
So what I built here--

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:49.020
I just want to explain
it a little bit.

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:52.980
So if you go to the
showcase in Moscone West,

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:54.990
and you go to the
Cloud Spanner showcase,

00:42:54.990 --> 00:42:59.070
we have one that illustrates the
manageability of Cloud Spanner.

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:00.840
And then, there's
station two, where

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:05.310
you can play with scaling the
number of nodes out and in,

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:08.160
and scaling the
throughput up and down.

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:10.980
And it shows you how
quickly Spanner actually

00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:14.160
adapts to changing
throughputs, as well as

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:17.900
how quickly nodes that you
add become available to serve

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:19.170
your workload.

00:43:19.170 --> 00:43:20.620
It's pretty amazing.

00:43:20.620 --> 00:43:24.060
So I've built this
app, or the back-end,

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:26.880
by having clients
running in Kubernetes.

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.630
And they are attaching
to a Pub/Sub session.

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:32.430
And then I can use
Pub/Sub to send a message

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:35.160
and instruct them how
much traffic to serve,

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:38.220
how much traffic to send
against Cloud Spanner--

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:39.660
so how much read
traffic, how much

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:42.930
write traffic, how much
time-bounded read traffic.

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:46.680
Now, I find that very powerful
because it is a very easy way

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:50.670
to have an interface to
communicate with, especially,

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:53.610
the agency that built the
station and the back-end

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:55.570
that I was building.

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:57.390
So in this case,
what I want to show

00:43:57.390 --> 00:44:00.710
is I set up a system
where I want to show you

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:05.760
the differences between a
multi-regional continental

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.560
and a multi-regional
global instance,

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:13.210
in terms of what the latencies
are that you're going to see.

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:14.780
So let's have a look here.

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:19.090
I'm going to change to, let's
say, multi-region global.

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:20.970
Now I'm going to
save these changes.

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:21.565
OK.

00:44:21.565 --> 00:44:22.760
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:22.760 --> 00:44:23.490
ROBERT KUBIS: Oh.

00:44:23.490 --> 00:44:26.290
Thank you very much.

00:44:26.290 --> 00:44:27.581
I apologize for that.

00:44:27.581 --> 00:44:28.080
OK.

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:32.070
So basically what you see
here, in the upper graph, is--

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:33.730
it's showing on this all right.

00:44:33.730 --> 00:44:39.322
So the yellow line is the
random reads coming from the US.

00:44:39.322 --> 00:44:43.200
No, the yellow is Asia--
random reads from Asia.

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:45.890
The blue ones, or the second
line from the top, is Europe,

00:44:45.890 --> 00:44:47.820
and the lowest one is US.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:51.170
So you can see the US random
reads for a global instance is

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:52.850
nine milliseconds.

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:54.260
These are point reads.

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:57.560
And for Asia, it's 165.

00:44:57.560 --> 00:44:59.270
And for Europe, it's 114.

00:44:59.270 --> 00:45:01.880
So these are strong reads, OK?

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:06.080
Now, if we look at time-bounded
ones, we see here, all of them

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:08.190
are below 10 milliseconds.

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:10.550
So this is the power
of time-bounded reads.

00:45:10.550 --> 00:45:12.900
These time-bounded reads
have a staleness up to 10

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:14.570
milliseconds--

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:17.720
10 seconds, apologies.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:20.690
They have staleness
up to 10 seconds.

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:24.976
But all of them are served
within 10 milliseconds.

00:45:24.976 --> 00:45:25.850
So that is the power.

00:45:25.850 --> 00:45:29.070
Especially if you want
to filter your data,

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:31.320
you can use it very nicely.

00:45:31.320 --> 00:45:36.110
Now, if we look at the
multi-region continental,

00:45:36.110 --> 00:45:38.600
what you see there is pretty
much very similar times.

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:40.550
But now, as you can
see, the random reads

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:43.190
are 185 milliseconds.

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:46.350
So we had, before,
like 140 milliseconds.

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:48.740
So the reason for
that is the data

00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:51.770
has always to be
shipped to my clients

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:54.130
in Asia because they don't
have a read-only copy.

00:45:54.130 --> 00:45:58.970
So even for these
point reads, it

00:45:58.970 --> 00:46:02.270
makes a 40-millisecond,
roughly, difference

00:46:02.270 --> 00:46:09.920
by having this read-only copy
in Asia, in the local region.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:13.810
Now, I want to show one more
thing, if it is working.

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:14.904
Let's see.

00:46:14.904 --> 00:46:16.880
Yep.

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:20.870
So here, I am displaying,
again, random writes.

00:46:20.870 --> 00:46:24.840
So we have here, writes from
the US-- about 30 milliseconds.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:27.440
This is going to a
regional instance.

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:30.140
From Asia, around 200
milliseconds, and from Europe,

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:34.700
about 130 milliseconds
round-trip time.

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:38.330
The sequential writes,
right now, it has started.

00:46:38.330 --> 00:46:39.460
It's about the same.

00:46:39.460 --> 00:46:41.420
Now, if I would put that up--

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:43.280
it takes a little bit of time--

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:46.460
you basically would see
that Spanner tops out

00:46:46.460 --> 00:46:50.850
at 35% of usage
in your instance,

00:46:50.850 --> 00:46:53.000
because I'm basically
always hitting the last bit.

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:56.899
So the throughput that I would
get for the sequential writes

00:46:56.899 --> 00:46:58.940
is much lower, even though
I have the same amount

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:02.031
of compute resources available.

